## Preliminaries: check your setup

Evaluate this cell after setup finishes. A **satisfiable** result showing `ready`
confirms that the bundled clingo is running inside the notebook. Each solve
closes its own native control; this check is safe to reevaluate.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../support/course.exs", __DIR__))
alias ASPCourse, as: Course
alias Clingo.Control

Clingo.solve!("ready. #show ready/0.") |> Course.display()
```

## Supply text, files and grounding functions

### What you will learn

Keep ASP as the modeling language while Elixir supplies data.

### Use copied symbols at the boundary

A grounding function receives decoded symbols and returns symbols. Here Elixir doubles the numeric argument. It is called while grounding, not once per candidate model during search.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!("value(@twice(6)). #show value/1.",
    context: %{"twice" => fn [number] -> Clingo.Symbol.number(number.value * 2) end},
    symbols: true
  )

[%{shown: ["value(12)"]}] = result.models
symbol = Clingo.Symbol.parse!("value(12)")
"value(12)" = Clingo.Symbol.format!(symbol)
Course.display(result)
```

### Load a file without an empty program string

The file example uses a temporary directory and cleans it up even if solving fails. Includes resolve relative to the input file. The first solve uses only files; the second adds a constraint as inline ASP.

```elixir
directory = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "learning-input-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
File.mkdir_p!(directory)

try do
  File.write!(Path.join(directory, "items.lp"), "item(oak;pine).")
  path = Path.join(directory, "select.lp")
  File.write!(path, ~S|#include "items.lp". 1 { chosen(Item) : item(Item) } 1. #show chosen/1.|)
  all = Clingo.solve!(files: [path], models: :all)
  restricted = Clingo.solve!(":- chosen(oak).", files: [path], models: :all)
  2 = length(all.models)
  [%{shown: ["chosen(pine)"]}] = restricted.models
  Course.display(restricted)
after
  File.rm_rf!(directory)
end
```

### Try it yourself

Change @twice(6) to @twice(9). Explain when the function runs and predict the shown atom.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The copied numeric symbol has value 9; the function returns number(18), so the shown result is value(18). It runs during grounding, not during each model callback.

</details>

## Choose between complete results and lazy samples

### What you will learn

Read search status, stream model snapshots and apply temporary assumptions.

### Distinguish a limit from exhausted search

A one-model limit finds an answer but leaves alternatives unexplored. Complete enumeration returns eight models for three independent choices. A lazy stream can stop after two without keeping the other snapshots.

```elixir
program = "{flag(1..3)}."
limited = Clingo.solve!(program, models: 1)
:satisfiable = limited.status
false = limited.exhausted
complete = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)
true = complete.exhausted
8 = length(complete.models)
sample = Clingo.models(program, models: :all) |> Enum.take(2)
2 = length(sample)
%{complete: length(complete.models), sample: Enum.map(sample, & &1.shown)}
```

### Constrain one solve with an assumption

An assumption requires a symbolic atom to be true or false for that solve. It does not add a persistent rule. Both snapshots remain ordinary Elixir data after the scoped control closes.

```elixir
answers =
  Clingo.with_control([models: :all], fn control ->
    Clingo.Control.add!(control, "{enabled}. #show enabled/0.")
    Clingo.Control.ground!(control)
    constrained = Clingo.Control.solve!(control, assumptions: [{"enabled", true}])
    unconstrained = Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
    {constrained, unconstrained}
  end)

{constrained, unconstrained} = answers
[%{shown: ["enabled"]}] = constrained.models
2 = length(unconstrained.models)
Course.display(unconstrained)
```

### Try it yourself

Change the true assumption to false and compare the following unconstrained solve.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The constrained model has an empty shown set. The later solve still has two models because assumptions are temporary.

</details>
